    "slug": "honey-trade-offers-modest-income-for-families-in-merka",
    "summary": "In Merka, a young man has turned to selling honey to support his family after his father fell ill. His daily earnings of about $10 highlight a growing local trade that helps poor households cope with economic hardships.",
    "content": "In the coastal town of Merka, 33-year-old Abukar Dayib Nuuh spends his days walking through neighborhoods with containers of honey, offering his goods door-to-door and at local shops. Since February, after his father became too sick to work, Abukar has been the primary provider for his parents and siblings, a family of eight.\n\nStarting with a loan of $150, Abukar now sells an average of four liters of honey daily, earning roughly $10. This income, while modest, has eased the financial pressure on his household, allowing them to cover basic needs. His story reflects a broader trend in the area, where honey trade is becoming a survival strategy for families facing unemployment and limited economic opportunities.\n\nThe demand for honey in Merka is driven by its use in traditional foods and medicine, offering a steady market for sellers like Abukar. Local traders often source honey from rural producers and sell it in urban centers, creating a supply chain that benefits both farmers and vendors.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this report underscores the resilience of communities in the face of hardship. It also highlights the potential for small-scale enterprises to provide livelihoods where formal employment is scarce. Supporting such initiatives, whether through remittances or investment, could help sustain these fragile economic activities and improve living conditions in towns like Merka.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/08/trade-in-honey-sweetens-livelihood-challenges-for-poor-families-in-merka/",
